# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Environment variables
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Copy to `.env` (git-ignored) and set only what you need — every value here is
# optional.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# This file is for SCALARS. The object you paste from a vendor console does not
# go here: it belongs in `config/<name>.json`, one file per integration.
# See `config/README.md`.
#
#   config/firebase.json   the Firebase config object   <- paste the {} there
#   .env                   single values, overrides     <- you are here
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Firebase Hosting
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hosting site id, used to generate `hosting/.firebaserc`. Defaults to the
# projectId in `config/firebase.json`, which is right unless you created extra
# sites under Firebase Console -> Hosting.
# FIREBASE_HOSTING_SITE=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Site links
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where this deployment's source lives. When set (here or in `config/site.json`),
# the app renders "view source" links — a GitHub icon in the header and a button
# on the home page. Left empty, none of them render, which is the right state
# for a fresh clone. CI derives it from the repository that is building, so a
# fork's deploy links to the fork, never to the template.
# SITE_REPO_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-key overrides — mainly for CI
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Each variable below overrides that one key of `config/firebase.json`, and each
# also works as a real environment variable with no `.env` on disk. That is how
# CI supplies credentials: store them as GitHub Actions secrets and export them
# in the workflow, no config file needed.
#
# authDomain and storageBucket are derived from projectId when left empty.
#
# FIREBASE_API_KEY=
# FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=
# FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
# FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=
# FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=
# FIREBASE_APP_ID=
# FIREBASE_MEASUREMENT_ID=
#
# The pattern generalises: a future integration named `foo` reads
# `config/foo.json` and takes `FOO_<KEY>` overrides.
